==============================================================================
  Mutation tests  (assertion-strength validation)
==============================================================================

  Strategy: replace each named fact's body with `{}` (no constraint),
  then re-run Alloy. Each listed assertion should now FAIL on the
  mutated model. If it still PASSes, the assertion is vacuous and
  must be strengthened.

  - MUTATE F_AppendOnlyAuditEntries  → runs/A-L1/mutated_F_AppendOnlyAuditEntries.als
    rationale: Clearing this fact allows Alloy to construct a world where two AuditEntries share the same (application, eventType) pair — an UPDATE-style overwrite. Both AppendOnly and FR_016_AuditAppendOnly require the pair to be unique; injecting a duplicate forces a counterexample.
    expected to FAIL: AppendOnly, FR_016_AuditAppendOnly
      - AppendOnly: FAIL (expected)
      - FR_016_AuditAppendOnly: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_OnePendingPerCustomer  → runs/A-L1/mutated_F_OnePendingPerCustomer.als
    rationale: Clearing this fact allows a customer to hold two simultaneous PendingReview applications, which violates FR-005 and the partial-unique-index invariant. The injection explicitly constructs this forbidden state, ensuring the predicate finds a counterexample.
    expected to FAIL: FR_005_OnePendingPerCustomer
      - FR_005_OnePendingPerCustomer: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_PermissionMatrix  → runs/A-L1/mutated_F_PermissionMatrix.als
    rationale: Clearing the closed-world matrix assignment lets Alloy freely populate PermMatrix.Allowed. Adding Customer -> PostDecision directly violates the LeastPrivilege predicate (which asserts that cell is denied) and exposes the missing staff-only guard checked by FR_015_StaffOnlyDecisionAudit.
    expected to FAIL: LeastPrivilege, FR_015_StaffOnlyDecisionAudit
      - LeastPrivilege: FAIL (expected)
      - FR_015_StaffOnlyDecisionAudit: PASS (vacuous?)

  - MUTATE F_AtMostOneDecisionPerApplication  → runs/A-L1/mutated_F_AtMostOneDecisionPerApplication.als
    rationale: Clearing this fact allows two Decision atoms to reference the same LoanApplication, modelling a race-condition win by two concurrent staff members. The injection forces exactly this forbidden state, causing all three assertions that prohibit dual decisions to find counterexamples.
    expected to FAIL: ConcurrencySafety, FR_012_AtMostOneDecisionPerApp, FR_011_DecisionImmutability
      - ConcurrencySafety: FAIL (expected)
      - FR_012_AtMostOneDecisionPerApp: FAIL (expected)
      - FR_011_DecisionImmutability: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_OwnershipBasedAccess  → runs/A-L1/mutated_F_OwnershipBasedAccess.als
    rationale: Clearing this fact removes the constraint that a Customer's GetApplicationByRef operation must target only their own application. The injection constructs a Customer operation that targets another customer's application, violating all three ownership/isolation/leakage assertions.
    expected to FAIL: OwnershipBasedAccess, FR_013_CustomerIsolation, NoInformationLeakage
      - OwnershipBasedAccess: FAIL (expected)
      - FR_013_CustomerIsolation: FAIL (expected)
      - NoInformationLeakage: FAIL (expected)

  - MUTATE F_AuditAttribution  → runs/A-L1/mutated_F_AuditAttribution.als
    rationale: Clearing this fact allows AuditEntries to carry a misattributed actor — e.g., a Submitted event attributed to a BankStaff member, or an Approved event attributed to a Customer. The injection forces the most egregious misattribution (a Submitted event attributed to staff), which is caught by AttributionCorrectness.
    expected to FAIL: AttributionCorrectness, FR_016_AuditAppendOnly
      - AttributionCorrectness: FAIL (expected)
      - FR_016_AuditAppendOnly: PASS (vacuous?)

